# Binaries
click-dog
click-dog-linux-*
click-dog-darwin-*
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary
*.test

# Output of the go coverage tool
*.out
*.prof
coverage.html

# Release/build artifacts
dist/
*.oci.tar

# Config files (keep only example)
config.yaml
*-config.yaml
click-dog.yaml

# Go workspace file
go.work

# Integration test output
testing/otel-output/*.jsonl

# MkDocs build output. site/ is the pre-rename name, kept ignored so a stale
# directory in an existing clone cannot dirty the tree.
target/
site/

# check-site-escape.sh probe dirs; only a killed run leaves one behind
docs/escape-probe.*

# Docker buildx scratch context
.docker-buildx/

# IDE
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# AI
.claude/
.agent-cache/
# ai/ orchestration workspace: only ai/prompts/ (and ai/README.md) are
# committed; per-session decision/finding/context files stay local.
ai/decisions/
ai/findings/
ai/extended-context/

# Loose maintainer scratch notes at the repo root. These carry unpublished
# product and commercial thinking, and nothing in the public-boundary check
# stops a stray root file: scripts/verify-public-boundary.sh denies known
# internal paths rather than allowing known public ones. One `git add -A`
# published "clickdog issues." in the v26.09.1-beta.1 tag. Keep scratch notes
# ignored so that cannot happen again.
/clickdog issues.
/*.notes

# The installer lives at deploy/install.sh and is published from there as a
# release asset. The same commit that added the scratch notes also left a
# stale copy of the released installer at the root; ignore the path so a
# downloaded `install.sh` cannot be committed over it again.
/install.sh
